Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

I like my Alphas, I have one at work for running big computational
electromagnetics codes and one at home for fiddling with (owned by me,
not my employers) and I basically agree with the points made in this
thread.  However, it would not be fair to the person posting the question
to overlook some of the cons.

The main problem with Linux on Alphas is that a lot of code is either
not available or somewhat unstable.  em86 helps sometimes, as does
the ability to run DEC Unix binaries (if you can get the libraries).

Some examples of codes not available for Alphas:

Netscape (em86 or DEC libraries can fix this)
WordPerfect
Wine
Soundblaster Live driver (lots of other sound cards are OK however)

Even having access to the source often doesn't help.  I recently needed
a WAV file editor.  I tried 8 different codes, some ran but crashed every few
minutes, some crashed when started and most wouldn't compile from source.
I never did get one working properly.

The upshot of this is that Alphas are great for running big number
crunching tasks (especially with the new Compaq compilers), but many
desktop applications are either unavailable or difficult to get running
reliably.  So if you want to use an Alpha as a desktop machine be prepared
for some frustration.

I should add that the problems with many codes does have the upside of
being somewhat more exciting and providing plenty of interesting
challenges :-).

ANTI-FLAME STATEMENT:- These comments are based on my own personal
experiences with my Alpha systems.  I am sure that there are Alpha users
who have never had any problems with any codes which they have installed
on their machines.  I am simply saying that either they have been lucky
or I have been unlucky.  I am not a hopeless newbee.  I have been using
Linux for several years and have had a number of small patches included
in the kernel source.

Thank you, 

-- 
Richard Simpson
Farnborough, Hants, Uk                 Fax: 01252 392118
rsimpson@ewrcsdra.Cut_This_Out.demon.co.uk
