> >>4GB of memory is also supported, but there are some ugly gotchas,
> >mainly due to PCI devices.  Most drivers do not understand physical
> >addresses >32 bits.
> 
> Which I guess says that there's still some 64 bit transition still to
> come...

Depends on your point of view: right now it just means that physical memory 
is currently limited to 2 gigabytes (can't trust the hardware for the 
32nd bit apparently) on PCI based systems running Linux.  Works fine on 
Digital UNIX right now, for example on the same hardware and Jay++'s working 
on making Linux do the right thing...

Virtual address space does not have these limits; all applications are already
running as fully 64 bit applications on Alpha.  So while drivers will need
updating to be used on large memory systems, applications just won't notice
at all (other than having more real memory to run in...).
				- Jim



