I think the 32/64-bit issue is a red herring.  For what it's worth,
Linux is 64-bit as seen from the applications but (from another thread)
supports only 31-bit *physical* addresses.  This only matters if you
buy more than 2GB of physical memory.

The quality of the optimizing compilers is more important.  Compaq plans
to release its compilers for Linux (ya-hoo!) and the Fortran is already
available as a beta.  You need C/C++ and it doesn't look like you can
wait, so go with True64.

