Greg Lindahl <lindahl@pbm.com> wrote:
>
>Go back and read the thread -- that's NOT what anyone said. The only
>thing Linux is missing for SPEC95fp is page coloring, which is (1) not
>a huge effect, and (2) going to be added at some point.

Actually, I doubt I'll _ever_ do page colouring.  It only makes sense on
architectures with limited cache associativity (ie direct mapped caches
and 2-way associative), and I don't expect any interesting CPU's to show
up with that particular braindamage in the forseeable future.  Alpha was
just about the last hold-out, and the 21264 fixes that to a large
degree. 

And page colouring does have a number of nasty downsides.  It made a lot
of sense 15 years ago when fortran with large numercal arrays and nicely
predictable cache behaviour was de-rigeur (and caches were almost always
direct-mapped), but I personally don't think it makes sense any more. 

		Linus



