You'll be encouraged to..

>On the other hand, if I can't make the code pretty (someone at Cygnus
>did page coloring a while back, but it wasn't pretty) or it is a small
>effect... then I'll chalk it up as a lesson on mm internals.

The cache colouring stuff I've seen so far had horrible problems with
memory fragmentation etc, but that may have been a implementation
problem.  So while they sometimes had noticeable improvements for some
stuff, the system really had problems with efficient memory usage etc
and basically had many worse downsides than the few successes it had. 

The biggest advantage of cache colouring to some people is that it makes
performance much more repeatable (regardless of any performance impact
in itself - sometimes it's a negative performance impact but people are
happy to just get the _same_ bad performance every time ;). 

Again, with higher associativity that advantage tends to also get lost
pretty quickly.

		Linus



