Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

While playing around with some portable high-precision timing code,
I have run into a rather weird effect in which the result of time(NULL)
actually _decreases_ momentarily. That means that a call to time() may
give a result of x, and a _subsequent_ call might give a result of x-1.
Needless to say, that wreaks havoc on my code, and probably is not too
good for any other time-related code, either.

The same problem occurs with the gettimeofday function, which gives more
information...

Here is a little C program to test it:

=========
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <sys/time.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
  struct timeval tv1,tv2;
  
  gettimeofday(&tv1,NULL);
  while (1) {
    gettimeofday(&tv2,NULL);
    if (tv1.tv_sec!=tv2.tv_sec) {
      printf("%d  %d.%06d<->%d.%06d\n",
	     tv2.tv_sec-tv1.tv_sec,
	     tv1.tv_sec,tv1.tv_usec,
	     tv2.tv_sec,tv2.tv_usec);
    }
    tv1=tv2;
  }
}
=========

When run on my Alphas (Kernel 2.2.0pre7ac4, libc-2.0.7, one machine a
433MHz 21164, the other a 533MHz 21164PC), the output will mostly consist
of lines starting with "1", as one should expect (and does indeed get
on linux.x86 all the time).

But every now and again, sometimes as often as twice a minute, sometimes
only every ten minutes or so, a line starts with "-1", indicating that time
just went backwards. 

Here is an example:
=======
1  933410708.999994<->933410709.000004
1  933410709.998043<->933410710.002138
-1  933410710.002138<->933410709.998180
1  933410709.999995<->933410710.000005
1  933410710.999994<->933410711.000005
=======

The actual "wrong" answer from gettimeofday is in the second line starting with
"1".

This shows an interesting aspects of the problem: The gettimeofday function 
simply "jumps the gun" by about 4 milliseconds, for no apparent reason.

Any ideas?

Bernie

