Hi, I can tell you the following because I myself have a PC164 motherboard.

The PC164 uses an ATX type power connector which any ATX type powersupply
will give you. The problem is that you neex an ATX power supply that has a
power switch hard wired into it because the PC164 board doesn't support the
soft power on feature that ATX brought on.

Two solutions, the one I use, or the one I thought I'd use:

1) The solution I thought I'd use.. The power supply that DEC used with the
board was designed by Quantum Power Labs at www.quantumpower.com . They are
sselling the unit for $90 though and not to individuals, or so I think...
They did fax me the schematics for the unit though.. how nice!

2) what I did. I just used a normal ATX power supply. All you have to do is
short pin 14 on the power connector (the soft-on pin) with ground when you
want your computer on. When it is no longer shorted the system will be off.
I'll let you play around with the rest of the details. I currently have the
pin shorted directly to ground with no resistance oin between and everything
work fine. The more resistance, the less current or voltage that's going to
the board, I havent' taken to the time to figure it out.

Well, I hope that helped!

    -Aravind Mikkilineni
       mikkilineni.1@osu.edu


