Johannes Nix <Johannes.Nix@mail.uni-oldenburg.de> writes:

> Who has experiences with the Compaq ES40 server running Linux with
> three or four CPU?

Probably few people other than the Compaq team which ported Linux to
them. They are fairly expensive and not a price/performance winner for
your typical AlphaLinux user...

> Which are alternatives for multiprocessor Alpha-SMP systems for heavy,
> realtime floating point calculations, with very little file I/O and
> tiny memory requirements (File I/O perhaps 500 kB/s and total memory
> typically less than 10 MB) ?

None, other than clusters or more specialized machines, which you seem
to want to avoid.

> Because we have
> the limitation that for many applications the total computing time
> should be not more than 20 milliseconds,

This is hard to do with a cluster, as you have probably realized.
However, if you use networking like Myrinet, you can pass short
messages in as few as 18 microseconds, which means you may be able to
use a cluster. It all depends on the details, of course. There are
other networking technologies which go as fast as 5 microseconds.

> Are there assembler-optimized floating-point vector libraries for
> Alpha processors ?

Yes; there are math libraries mentioned in the FAQ. In particular
there are vectorized routines which do many sqrt() or other operations
at once for a substantial speed increase. There are also BLAS
routines. The Compaq XML libraries are supposed to be available in a
few months.

-- g




