Michael Walter <Michael.Walter@physik.uni-freiburg.de> writes:

> and get spurious Floating point exception with properly tested
> source code, which runs on SGI-IRIX as well as on Pentium-PC's
> with g++ version 

That isn't proof. The SGI and g++ by default on Pentium PC's uses the
IEEE mode which allows infinity etc. By default on an Alpha, infinity
and NaN cause a fault. I would suggest using gdb to figure out exactly
what the fault is. You should also try the -mieee option to gcc.

A common, different problem is that the default Alpha math libraries
generate IEEE extreme values even when -mieee is not specified. You
can get around that by using the Compaq math libraries, which never
return extreme values.

-- g




