Hi,
after 4 days of experimenting and fsck'ing we're getting a bit frustrated
about using Linux/Alpha as a NFS-Server. The hardware is about 20 months
old and was used as a mixed NT/Alpha workstation, so it had no high
uptimes (best was ~30days Linux), but now it even achieves not more than 
90 minutes.

The board is an EB164, 500MHz 21164, 128MB RAM (PS/2 SIMMs).
Symbios 8600 SCSI, 2 SCSI-disks, 1 SCSI-CDROM, 3 IDE-disks.

We've tried the following setups:

Kernel 2.2.1, 2.2.3, 2.2.10-ac10
Suse 6.1beta, Redhat 5.2 (originally on the system)
ISA-VGA or PCI-VGA (but only console, no X)
NE2K-PCI (10Mbit), 21143 (with the latest tulip driver), 3c905 (100MBit)
DMA-IDE on/off (sometimes even no IDE mounted)
External power supply to the drives

Under ALL combinations, the system simply freezes after an arbitray amount
of time, but it takes never longer than ~90min.
It can withstand 90min of high NFS/IDE/SCSI transfer (load >5)
and freezes then, sometimes it freezes immediately after the first root-login.
There are no console-messages (a la CIA machine check) at this time.

So I suspect that the hardware seems to have a bug. Could it be a
RAM problem? I know that the board has has ECC, but are there any messages when
recoverable errors occur? What is the behavior for non-recoverable RAM errors?

Waiting for some suggestions....

