Scott <scott4000@technologist.com> writes:

>     I realize that Tru64 is expensive, and it has a good C compiler.  But
> what I am interested in is the performance aspects of the kernel.

It depends on what you're doing with it.

If you're using 1 or 2 cpus, the main way Linux hurts you is that it
doesn't have page coloring, and it probably doesn't swap as well as
Tru64 (most people rarely swap). But Linux has faster syscalls and the
lowest network latencies in the industry. Tru64 has better support for
high-end devices, but Linux has more breadth on the low end.

At larger numbers of cpus, especially with lots of I/O, Tru64 probably
does much better.

Btw, Compaq C for AlphaLinux is supposed to be beta-testing this
summer. Compaq Fortran for AlphaLinux is already in beta-test.

-- g




