The sx164 AlphaBIOS is causing me headaches. I hope someone has solved
this:

I am running AlphaBIOS 5.66 (the latest). It purports to do a
multi-loader itself, but fails for many and sundried reasons.

Step 1) I partition the drive within the AlphaBIOS. It really wants
you to install NT, so you make a bunch of FAT partitions, including
the tiny one for MILO that they recommend. Make that the first
partition.

Step 2) Perform all the right stuff with the floppy images as
described in
http://www.redhat.com/corp/support/manuals/RHL-6.0-Manual/alpha-inst/booklet/doc016.html
(Section 2.5.2, which relates exactly to this BIOS, as opposed to the
older, but probably simpler, ARC)

Step 3) Install Linux normally. This involves using Disk Druid or
FDISK to re-partition the drive with native Linux partitions (except
for the MILO bootloader partition which you will need later to boot
from hard disk). Then install Linux on the new partitions normally.

Step 4) DISASTER: The AlphaBIOS, on reboot, doesn't recognize anything
about the hard disk, except that it exists. It claims the drive is
off-line and has no partitions, nor does it let you even try to make
one. Basically, it's pouting that someone overwrote its partition
table. You can only boot from floppy. Of course, you *can* boot the
Linux you just installed, as long as you boot from floppy, and the
Linux kernel can find the partitions just fine. But who wants to boot
from floppy forever?

Step 5) How to make the drive recognizable to AlphaBIOS again? Simple:
dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/hda bs=512 count=1 , simply the nastiest thing
you can do to a drive without throwing it through the window, namely
destroy the partition table and pretty much lose the entire
installation you just did. But the AlphaBIOS will recognize the drive
again. But it will have the same problem if you do it again.

So, how has anyone dealt with this? Actually, I'd prefer to dual-boot
Linux and NT, but if I can't use the Linux partitioning tools, what am
I to do? Right now, it looks like the only solution is to put in a
second hard disk, leave NT and MILO on the first, but have Linux on
the second, where I don't have to care whether AlphaBIOS can find it.

Has anyone else had this problem? What's the solution to dueling
partition software? How can I make the Ext2 partitions without
AlphaBIOS going into a snit?

Thanks in advance,
Bill




