Bill Zettler (bzettler@zettler.com) wrote:
: Won't work. As I say, once I've even touched the disk with either fdisk or Disk Druid, the
: AlphaBIOS refuses to even recognize the partition table.

Very weird. I did what you claim that "won't work" many times on different
alphas and it worked.  When "mass preparing" disks I also used sfdisk
in a script to partition disks and this worked fine as well.

In any case - AlphaBIOS (ARC BIOS, whatever your flavour of the week of
firmware happens to be named) includes in most (all?) version tools for
creating and formating FAT partitions (some formatting tools in some
versions are actually somewhat broken but creating partitions works
always - AFAIK).

To avoid exchanges like in this thread, and guessing what possibly went
wrong and why, we (i.e. Hard Data) always recommend that with a "virgin"
disk you start from creating and formatting some 2-3 Meg FAT partition.
You can later use it for milo and autobooting, or you may delete it,
does not matter.  Use fdisk to create all other partitions as firmware
assumes that you will be running NT and is likely do to something
totally brain-dead.  This always worked without any problems; even
for dual boot NT/Linux alphas (once in a blue moon a beast like that
happens).

  Michal




