Peanuts it wasn't. Expensive it wasn't either - we got a better price for
DPW 500au's (which includes DU licence) from ex-Digital than for 21164
clones of a comparable speed with no O/S (read Linux) from Aspen. Weak
Canadian dollar certainly helped in that. Our overall cost per node was
cdn$3680 - this includes DPWs themselves, network hardware, additional
disks for the server node, installation costs and all software (including
application software). This does not include cost of infrastructure,
ongoing ownership, or system administration costs - but then, I do not 
think that the Avalon figure includes any of that either. You'll find
more details on exactly how the money were allocated at:

http://www.cobalt.chem.ucalgary.ca/cobalt/poster/

If my math is right, the Avalon's US$313K [1] per 140 nodes (each roughtly
identical to what we have) translates to cad$3316 using the '98 average
CAD/USD exchange rate (1.4831 [2]). This is slightly (10%) less than what 
we paid, but given that our codes run about 30% faster with Digital's 
compiler (for which we'd got RTU's as part of our deal with Digital) I
think we cut at least as good a deal as the Avalon guys. 

Obviously, the tradeoffs are different for them and for us: they could
afford to hire highly qualified full-time staff to support their operation
(they list six people among full-time support staff[3]; I have no idea
how much of that works on Avalon proper though), while 90% of software work
on Cobalt was done my myself on a part-time basis. At the same time, we
can (could?) get better deals with Digital thanks to their generous 
educational discuscounts while LANL can't. (At the same time, I think that
LANL still gets very good discounts due to their sheer size. I am sure
they can get a much better deal with Carera than we ever would).

: cost of Avalon and Avalon is on Top 500 list. 

Which proves? The presence or absence of a system in Top-500 is more an
indication of willingness to spend the effort (and money) on running the 
tests than of the system's performance as such. For a system which is
not ever going to run applications remotely similar to Linpack-peak
(and we do not) getting into Top-500 is a rather pointless excersize...

: If you want to say that DU is a very good Unix implementation than
: I totally agree.  If you want to say that it is perfect - than I know
: too much about it to know that this is very far from true. :-)

In your original post you asserted that it is much more expensive to
build a Digital Unix-based cluster than an equivalent Linux cluster.
I was responding solely to that (untrue) assertion, and wasn't trying
to pass any value judgement whatsoever.

: to them this would be hard to do without sources.  Undoubtely if you
: part with enough of cash then you can buy Tru64 sources as well - but
: I do not think that this is a realistic proposition for the most
: involved.  

I was given to understand that source code licence for Tru64 is not
expensive if the licensee is an educational institution and will
not use the source code for commercial purposes. The amount of US$200
(yes, two hundred) was mentioned as the licence fee; I do not know
how accurate that amount is. You may wish to contact your Decpaq
representative on this.

: You would probably be better of financing a development of Linux 
: alpha compilers.

I do not think so. We can get superiour Digital's compilers here and now,
for a fairly reasonable fee. (And the availability of lower quality but
free compiler certainly helps to keep the price of Digital's compiler
lower than it could have been otherwise). Since we need to do our research
*now*, and not some years in the future when gcc may (or may not) catch
up with Digital's alpha compilers, we are substantially better off paying
Digital ... but YMMV, as usual.

/Serge.P

