Subject: found a clue of the knfsd trouble on Alpha/Linux from RedHat-6.0
Date: 1999/07/13
Message-ID: <378A11C1.B02280EF@nospam.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 500146735
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Organization: KJIST, KwangJu, KOREA
X-Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=EUC-KR
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

I realized that
knfsd on Alpha/LInux from RedHat-6.0 behaves differently from 
that on Intel/Linux from RedHat-6.0.
According to the difference between the file on two platforms, 
Version 3 rpc.mountd is running on Intel/Linux,
while rpc.mountd of an old version is running on Alpha/Linux by 
default.  Indeed, the offending line in /etc/rc.d/init.d/nfs
reads

	daemon rpc.mountd --no-nfs-version 3

while it is simply "daemon rpc.mountd" on Intel/Linux.

There is a memory leak from rpc.mountd on Alpha/Linux for some
reason, and I would like to correct the problem.  What would happen
if I simply get rid of "--no-nfs-version 3" from the file?
Why did RedHat turn off nfs-version 3?

Thanks.

-- 
G. Hugh Song



