While less and less frequent, it used to be the method of choice to develop code
for various embedded avionics computers using cross-compilers on a mainframe.
Included as part of that process was extensive weapon delivery algorithm
development using the mainframe and (if required) submission to a Cray for
"batch" processing.  This was because it had the horsepower required and because
the esoteric tools only existed for that platform.  Needless to say, the
developers are much happier now using the latest tools available.  Still, there
is no objective evidence that the products themselves (same embedded processors,
new software functionality) are of higher quality or being developed faster and
more efficiently using the latest tools and methods.

I've even developed pseudo-GUI user-interfaces for some specific limited-use
mainframe applications (most don't realize that the 3270 datastream supports
full-fledged graphical interaction), so some of us at least have done just about
everything possible with just about every computing platform at our disposal
over the years.  I wouldn't discount any specific category of problems from
being solved on a mainframe and so I remain interested in these performance
comparisons.

