>I suspect the DEC compiler is optimized for RISC machines with a large
>register set.  I wouldn't expect too much performmance improvement over
>gcc on a x86, due to the different architecture

The majority of optimizations our compilers provide are not
architecture-specific.  True, some optimizations aren't useful with
small register sets, but many others are - the instruction that isn't
issued because it isn't needed is the fastest of all.  As Greg Lindahl
points out, our X86 product for Windows, DIGITAL Visual Fortran,
produces very good code indeed.



