Gary <Gary.L.Scott@lmtas.lmco.com> writes:

> I think that there are faster microprocessors at least adverts indicate
> that they are faster at over 1080 MIPs (is a MIP a MIP a MIP?).

This is straying somewhat off-topic, but to briefly answer your
question, no, a MIP is not necessarily a MIP.  Its not even
particularly close.  I take it that you are not familliar with the
vernacular version of what the acronym MIPS stands for.  It is
Meaningless Indicator of Processor Speed.  (There are also some other
variations, but they tend to have the same general drift).  The
primary use of MIPS is for marketing departments to cite.  There are
many notorious examples of completely misleading MIPS figures.  No, I
don't have citations handy.  If you want an indicator of processor
speed, it is hard to do much worse than MIPS.  I suppose you could
just cite the chip frequency - that's about the only measure I recall
seeing that is worse.

(I have no idea about the relative speeds of the machines alluded to;
I just know that looking at cited MIPS figures isn't a very good way
to guage).

-- 
Richard Maine
maine@altair.dfrc.nasa.gov



