I see that someone came up with a counter-example where using unnecessary 
code actually sped up a computation.  I had forgotten about such 
situations.  Yes, sometimes adding strictly unnecessary NOP (no operation) 
instructions in strategic locations actually speeds up execution at the 
price of increasing code size.  Perhaps today's lesson should be, then:
 
"Grasshopper, concise proverbs go only so far in describing the complexity 
of truth."
 
