Toon Moene <toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl> writes:

> Dying hardware :-(
> 
> Compiling is a deterministic process (at least when using gcc :-)  You
> cannot compile a given source file "50 % of the time".

Strangely enough, compiling things on Linux 2.2.10 (both -linus and
-ac4) gave me exactly these sorts of non-deterministic errors, whereas
2.2.7 does not.  I know of at least one other person who has had the
same experience.  It's one of those things that shouldn't happen, but
does... possibly some sort of weird race condition or something (which
is about the only thing that could account for the randomness of the
failure)

If you can get 2.2.7 (with the net/ipv4/ip_options.c DoS patch of
course!) to compile, then you might be all right from that point on.

And yes, the 2.2.10 source code compiles flawlessly for me when
running 2.2.7 :-)

-- 
You do{} stuff.  Then it's $done.  Then you die().



