C.Simmendinger wrote:

> > > test-run:g77 -O3 -fexpensive-optimizations -fno-emulate-complex
> > > (egcs-1.1.1/cpml): 432s
> > > test-run: fort -O4 :  123 s

> > How about: -funroll-loops for egcs ?

> test-run:g77 -O3 -fexpensive-optimizations -fno-emulate-complex
> -funroll-loops (egcs-1.1.1/cpml): 420sHm, better, but still a mile off.
> BTW:The above result is by no means a general statement (!),
> i've seen egcs/g77 performing rather well too.

Normally, code that runs 3.5 faster with Compaq Fortran as opposed to
g77 has some feature in its hot spot that makes it immediately clear
where the speed difference comes from.

However, given that you use -fno-emulate-complex, I deduce:

1. Your code is complex-arithmetic-bound.

2. It is DOUBLE COMPLEX (on an Alpha egcs-1.1.2 would almost certainly
   fail with -fno-emulate-complex if your source contained single
   precision complex (gcc-2.95, the successor of egcs-1.1.2 will have
   -fno-emulate-complex as default, because we believe all the remaining
   problems with complex arithmetic in the common backend have been
   solved).

So, to make a stab at it:

1. Complex division, square root and abs are all computed via run-time
   library routines instead of by inline code.  gcc-2.95 will inline
   complex division.

2. You do not happen to have an expression like z ** 2. in your code ?
   (this is my favourite:  z ** 2. will be computed as exp(2.0*log(z)),
   whereas z ** 2 will be computed as z * z).

> Anyway i'll benchmark a few more of our codes plus the lapack now.
> DEC against g77 against fort:
> And to be completely unfair i'll use Goto's assembler routines.
> (against the DEC alpha).

Yeah, no compiler can beat hand-optimized assembler on the LAPACK
routines.

-- 
