> Well, in my experience the problem with egcs is like this:If you can keep
> the data in the cache, egcs does a pretty good job.
> If not then not.
> Just try a very simple thing, a gemm, complex or not. Run that unoptimized
> (no cache blocking etc) through egcs. The performance then is
> limited by memory bandwidth (as it should). Do the same with
> the GEM (DEC) compiler: It's 3-4 times faster.

Yes, but the DEC compilers I know of only do that sort of loop
transformation at optimisation level -O5 and you used -O4 (at least,
that is what you wrote).

Or do newer DEC compilers already perform this optimisation at lower -On
?

I don't think you will see this optimisation any time soon in egcs/gcc,
because it is very hard to do right at the level of detail the loop
optimisations take place.

> > 1. Complex division, square root and abs are all computed via run-time
> >    library routines instead of by inline code.  gcc-2.95 will inline
> >    complex division.
> 
> -O3 should inline that (!?). I also use the cpml.

No, -O3 only inlines subprograms earlier in the same source file, not
run-time library routines (this is due to the way inlining works in
gcc/g77:  The backend "saves" the internal representation of small
routines until the end of processing an entire file, to see if there's a
spot where they might be inlined).

HTH,

